# acos-nemesis-protocol — Product Requirements Document

Version 1.0 · 2026-10-08 · Decision ledger: `prd/DECISIONS.md` · Trace: `prd/TRACE.json`

## 1. Purpose

Nemesis is the companion that rides along while code is being written. Every code chunk —
each `Write`, `Edit`, `NotebookEdit`, and every file a `Bash` command changes — is judged
against the WHOLE Ten-Star Security Protocol (the **Prime Security Checks, PSC**: S0–S8,
every checklist, every record) and against the **Prime Functionality Checks (PFC)**: (a) bug
detection reading the code base, (b) fixing what is found, (c) proving the fix breaks nothing,
(d) no drift from the PRD or the goal, (e) no new bug introduced. Findings are fixed by an
automatic ladder that never hands code to Zee. Nothing is marked done while anything is open.
It runs beside Oracle mode (Oracle judges harm to the machine; Nemesis judges the rulebook
and the plan), through hooks that fire on every tool call, so every builder skill and agent
Zee has is covered without knowing Nemesis exists.

## 2. Non-goals

- Nemesis is not a post-build audit. (`acos-robust-code-review` and `security-audit` remain
  for that.)
- Nemesis never asks Zee to fix code. It may hand him a product decision in plain language.
- Nemesis does not replace the Oracle, autopilot, or the eternity protocol. It coexists.
- Nemesis does not read the restricted reviewer-rules directory or modify `.claude/agents/`.

## 3. Vocabulary

- **Task**: a unit of work (feature, slice, ticket) with its own S0–S8 records.
- **Chunk / edit**: one `Write`/`Edit`/`NotebookEdit` call, or one file changed by `Bash`.
- **Judge**: the Nemesis daemon's Opus session, read-only, no chat transcript.
- **Ledger**: the on-disk, append-only issue and step records under `.nemesis/`.
- **Rung**: a position on the escalation ladder (1–8).
- **Green**: the last state where the full S3 scan was clean.

## 4. Requirements

### 4.A Enrollment and scope

### R-001 — Enroll a project and install the hooks
Decisions: D-02, D-18, D-24.
`nemesis enroll [<projectDir>]` creates `.nemesis/` (`config.json`, `state/`, `tasks/`,
`issues/`, `records/`, `snapshots/`, `holdout/`, `reports/`), detects the toolchain, writes
`.gitignore` entries for `state/`, `snapshots/`, `holdout/*/pack`, and installs four hooks into
the project's `.claude/settings.local.json` — `PreToolUse` (Write|Edit|NotebookEdit|Bash),
`PostToolUse` (Write|Edit|NotebookEdit|Bash), `Stop`, `UserPromptSubmit` — idempotently
(running twice adds nothing). Each hook is invoked by the absolute bun path with a shell
fallback that FAILS CLOSED for PreToolUse (deny on crash) and fails open for the others.
Acceptance: after enroll, the settings file contains exactly one copy of each Nemesis hook;
`.nemesis/config.json` exists; a project with no `.claude/` directory (the OKOA Investor
Portal) is enrolled successfully.

### R-002 — Scope: what is judged and what is never touched
Decisions: D-17, D-36.
`config.json` lists `source_globs` (default: everything under the project) and
`exclude_globs` (always: `.nemesis/**`, `.worktree/**`, `node_modules/**`, `.git/**`,
`memory/handoffs/**`, `**/acos-token-monitor/**`, `.claude/scripts/eternity*`,
`.claude/scripts/_autopilot_eternity.py`, the restricted reviewer-rules directory,
`.claude/agents/**`). Excluded paths are never sent to the judge, never scanned, never
snapshotted. The reviewer-rules directory is never read by any Nemesis component;
`.claude/agents/` is never written.
Acceptance: `nemesis scope <path>` prints `in-scope` or `excluded (<rule>)`; every default
exclusion resolves as excluded.

### R-003 — Rulebook has one source
Decisions: D-30, D-06.
`rulebook/SECURITY-PROTOCOL.md` is a byte copy of the OKOA Investor Portal's
`design/security/SECURITY-PROTOCOL.md`. `scripts/build-rulebook.ts` parses it into
`rulebook/rulebook.json` (principles, S0–S8 with their bullet points, the twelve questions,
the severity table, checklists 3.1–3.14 with item ids like `3.2.3`, the four record
templates, Part 6) and records `source_sha256`. `rulebook.json` is never hand-edited;
`nemesis selftest` fails if its `source_sha256` does not match the markdown.
Acceptance: rulebook.json has 12 principles, 9 steps, 12 questions, 4 severity rows, 14
checklists, 4 templates; `source_sha256` equals `sha256(SECURITY-PROTOCOL.md)`.

### 4.B Activation and loop

### R-004 — Activate, deactivate, and run the keep-going loop
Decisions: D-05, D-19, D-20, D-21.
`nemesis on "<goal>" [--prd <file>]` writes `.nemesis/state/active.json` (goal, PRD path,
started_at, session), starts the Nemesis daemon, and — if no autopilot continuation loop is
active for this session — starts one via `python3 .claude/scripts/autopilot-activate.py on`
with the same goal. `nemesis off` stops the daemon's use for this project, clears the active
state, and stops the loop only if Nemesis started it. A vague goal (`looksVague` rule:
under four words or placeholder shapes) is refused with exit 3. Oracle mode may be on or
off; Nemesis never changes Oracle's state.
Acceptance: `on` then `status` shows active with the goal; `off` then `status` shows
inactive; `on "finish the work"` exits 3.

### R-005 — Hooks are inert when Nemesis is off or the path is out of scope
Decisions: D-01, D-02.
Every hook exits with `allow` (or no output) in well under 100 ms when `active.json` is
absent, or the file is excluded, or the tool is not one of the four. No daemon call is made.
Acceptance: hook invoked with no active state prints an allow decision and makes no socket
connection.

### 4.C Start phase (S0, S1, the contract)

### R-006 — No edit before the threat sketch and the design review
Decisions: D-21, D-24, D-40.
`nemesis task start <id> --title "<t>"` opens a task. `nemesis task sketch <id> <file>`
validates a Part-4.1 threat sketch (task, data_touched non-empty with class in
public|internal|confidential|sensitive, actors, six STRIDE lines each non-empty, abuse_case
of at least two sentences, checklists non-empty and each a valid 3.x id) and records S0 with
the artifact hash. `nemesis task design <id> <file>` validates a Part-4.2 design review
(data_flow, authorization_matrix non-empty, rls_rules, typed_errors containing at least
PERMISSION_DENIED and NOT_FOUND, logging with `never_log` non-empty, ai_use present or
"none", reviewer) and records S1. The PreToolUse hook DENIES any in-scope edit while no task
is open or S0/S1 is missing; the deny reason names the exact command to run next.
Acceptance: edit with no task → deny naming `nemesis task start`; with task but no sketch →
deny naming `task sketch`; with both → proceeds to judgment. A sketch missing one STRIDE
line is refused with the field named.

### R-007 — The contract travels with every verdict
Decisions: D-21, D-23, D-07.
Every judge request carries: the goal, the PRD excerpt relevant to the task (the task's
`prd_refs` section ids, or the whole PRD if under 20k chars), the threat sketch, the design
review, the rulebook (twelve questions, severity table, the S0-selected checklists in full,
the ids and titles of all others), the open issues for the file, the prior verdicts for the
file, and the PFC lens definition.
Acceptance: the daemon's request log for a verdict contains all of these fields.

### 4.D Per-edit gate (S2 — the line rule)

### R-008 — Every in-scope edit is judged before it lands
Decisions: D-03, D-13, D-15, D-01.
The PreToolUse hook, for `Write`/`Edit`/`NotebookEdit` on an in-scope file, computes the
candidate file (applies `old_string→new_string`, or the full `content`) in memory, derives a
unified diff against the file on disk, and sends diff + full candidate + the contract
(R-007) to the daemon. The chat transcript is never read and never sent. The judge may read
the repository (read-only tools) to inspect callers, tests, tables, and policies.
Acceptance: the daemon request contains `diff`, `candidate`, `path`, and no `context` or
`transcript` field; the judge session is spawned with only Read, Grep, Glob tools.

### R-009 — Verdict shape and what the writer is told
Decisions: D-04, D-09, D-07.
A verdict is JSON: `{"decision":"allow"|"deny","findings":[{"id","severity":"critical|high|medium|low","lens":"PSC"|"PFC","checklist":"3.2.3"|"Q7"|"PFC-d","where","what","root_cause","fix_hint"}],"history_assessment":{"stuck":bool,"recommended_rung":1..6,"why"}}`.
On deny, the hook returns the findings and the current rung instruction as the reason, so the
writer can act without reading anything else. On allow with zero findings, the edit proceeds.
Acceptance: a stubbed deny verdict produces a hook reason containing each finding's `what`
and `fix_hint` and the words `rung <n>`.

### R-010 — Findings become issues with history
Decisions: D-10, D-11, D-37.
Each finding opens (or re-attaches to) an issue `SEC-<n>` (PSC) or `FUN-<n>` (PFC) in
`.nemesis/issues/<id>.json` with: severity, lens, checklist, where, what, root_cause, the
task id, status, `rung`, and `attempts[]` (timestamp, diff hash, verdict id, outcome). A
re-denied finding on the same `where`+`checklist` appends an attempt rather than opening a
duplicate. Issue files are append-only in their history arrays.
Acceptance: two denials of the same finding yield one issue with two attempts.

### R-011 — Allowed edits leave an S2 record
Decisions: D-24.
On allow, the hook records `{edit_id, path, candidate_sha256, verdict_id}` in the task's
`steps.json` under S2 via the CLI library, and the PostToolUse hook later matches the written
file's hash to it.
Acceptance: after an allowed Write, `steps.json` S2 contains the file's sha256.

### R-012 — Fixes are judged like any edit; there is no bypass input
Decisions: D-35.
The hook accepts no flag, env var, or marker that skips judgment in a non-test-mode project.
A fix for an issue is an ordinary edit and is judged with the issue's history attached.
Acceptance: grep of the hook code shows the only skip conditions are the judge-child token
(R-040) and `test_mode` (R-051).

### 4.E Post-edit (S3) and Bash-written code

### R-013 — Code written through Bash is caught
Decisions: D-16, D-03.
PreToolUse on `Bash` snapshots the in-scope tree (path → sha256 index, cached and refreshed
incrementally). PostToolUse on `Bash` diffs the tree; every changed in-scope file is judged
(R-008 payload, with the whole file as the candidate and the diff from the snapshot) and
scanned (R-014). Findings are returned as a PostToolUse `block` with reason and become
issues.
Acceptance: a Bash heredoc that writes an in-scope file produces a judge call and, with a
stubbed deny, a block containing the finding.

### R-014 — The S3 mechanical scan after every change
Decisions: D-07, D-13, D-24, D-06.
PostToolUse on `Write`/`Edit`/`NotebookEdit`/`Bash` runs the whole-repo scan: type-check,
lint (security rules where the linter has them), tests (the suite; positive and negative),
secret scan (diff and repository), dependency audit, policy check (for SQL/migration files:
new table without `enable row level security`, new view without `security_invoker`), and
data tests (sensitive-value patterns — SSN/TIN, full card/bank numbers, bearer tokens — in
log statements, error messages, fixtures). Each check records `pass|fail|skipped` with
captured output in `.nemesis/records/scans/<n>.json` and in the task's `steps.json` S3.
`skipped` is NEVER clean. Failures open issues (`FUN-` for test/type/lint, `SEC-` for
secret/policy/data/dependency) and are returned as a `block` reason.
Acceptance: in a fixture with a failing test, PostToolUse returns block naming the test; in a
fixture with no test runner, S3 records `tests: skipped` and `nemesis status` shows the task
as not clean.

### R-015 — Toolchain detection and additive configuration
Decisions: D-13, D-02.
Detection order: `package.json` scripts (`typecheck`, `lint`, `test`) → `tsconfig.json`
(`tsc --noEmit`) → biome/eslint → vitest/jest/`bun test`; `Cargo.toml` → `cargo check`,
`cargo clippy`, `cargo test`; `pyproject.toml` → `ruff`, `pytest`. Secret scan: built-in
patterns always, plus `gitleaks` if installed. Dependency audit: `bun audit` / `npm audit` /
`cargo audit` / `pip-audit` when available, else `skipped`. `config.json` may ADD commands
(`extra_checks`) but cannot remove or replace the built-ins.
Acceptance: detection on a bun+tsconfig fixture yields tsc + bun test; `extra_checks` run
after the built-ins; a config attempting to disable a built-in is ignored with a warning.

### R-016 — The PFC lens inside every verdict
Decisions: D-07, D-23.
The judge evaluates, for every chunk: (a) correctness against the task's design and the
PRD acceptance criteria; (c) impact on callers and tests (reading them); (d) drift — any
behavior, endpoint, field, or dependency the PRD/goal did not ask for is a `PFC-d`
finding; (e) new defects introduced by the chunk compared to the file before it. (b) fixing
is the ladder (R-018).
Acceptance: a stubbed verdict with `lens: PFC`, `checklist: PFC-d` opens a `FUN-` issue
with that checklist.

### 4.F Issues, the ladder, closure

### R-017 — The issue ledger commands
Decisions: D-10, D-37.
`nemesis issue list [--open]`, `issue show <id>`, `issue close <id>` (R-025), and
`issue history <id>`. Statuses: `open`, `fixing` (a judged-clean edit landed on the issue's
file; closure proof still owed), `verifying` (reproduction test registered), `reverted-open`,
`closed`, `decided`. No command deletes an issue; no command sets `closed` except `issue close`
passing R-025; `decided` is set only by `issue decide` (R-024).
Acceptance: `issue list --open` after two findings prints both ids with severity and rung.

### R-018 — The escalation ladder, driven by the judge, not a counter
Decisions: D-04, D-09, D-08.
Rungs: (1) writer rewrites from the deny reason; (2) root cause named by the judge, writer
fixes the cause; (3) fresh-context fixer agent; (4) different model family second opinion;
(5) design redo (S1 re-derived); (6) revert to green, keep open, continue; (7) task-end
retry from rung 3 with full history; (8) goal-end product report. Rungs 1→6 advance ONLY
when the judge's `history_assessment.stuck` is true with a `recommended_rung`; the CLI
records the judge's `why`. No attempt counter advances a rung. Rungs 7 and 8 are position
rungs triggered by task end and goal end. `nemesis ladder <issue>` prints the current rung
and its exact instruction.
Acceptance: three denials with `stuck:false` leave the rung at 1; one denial with
`stuck:true, recommended_rung:3` sets rung 3 and records the why.

### R-019 — Rung 3: a fresh-context fixer
Decisions: D-12, D-09.
The rung-3 instruction (in the Stop/Prompt directive) tells the writer to spawn
`Task(developer)` with ONLY: the issue file (which carries every attempt and the judge's
reasons), the task records, and the repository path — no conversation history. Its edits are
judged like any other.
Acceptance: `nemesis ladder <issue>` at rung 3 prints the Task instruction with the issue
file path and the sentence "do not pass the conversation".

### R-020 — Rung 4: a different model family
Decisions: D-09.
`nemesis second-opinion <issue>` calls `.claude/scripts/run-external-agent.py` with the
provider from `config.json` (`second_opinion_provider`, resolved through
`.acos/config/providers.yaml`), the issue file, and the relevant files, and stores the
returned analysis and proposed patch in the issue. A Claude agent applies the patch (judged
as any edit). If no provider is configured, the issue records `rung4: unavailable (no
provider)` and the ladder proceeds to rung 5 — never silently skipped.
Acceptance: with no provider, `second-opinion` exits 0, records `unavailable`, and `ladder`
shows rung 5 next.

### R-021 — Rung 5: redo the design
Decisions: D-09.
`nemesis task redesign <task> <issue> <file>` marks the current S1 artifact as superseded
(kept), accepts a new design review file (validated as in R-006), records it as S1 v2, and
re-opens the task's S2/S3 steps for the affected files.
Acceptance: after redesign, `steps.json` shows S1 with two versions and S2 reset.

### R-022 — Rung 6: revert to green, keep the issue open
Decisions: D-09, D-39.
After every clean S3 the CLI writes a green snapshot (changed in-scope files since the last
snapshot, with hashes). `nemesis revert <issue>` restores the files named in the issue from
the latest green snapshot, records the revert in the issue (status `reverted-open`), and
leaves the issue open. Nemesis never rewrites git history or makes commits.
Acceptance: modify a file, run `revert`, file content equals the snapshot; issue status is
`reverted-open`.

### R-023 — Rung 7: every open issue is retried at task end
Decisions: D-09, D-10.
`nemesis task end` (R-032) with open issues does not close the task; it sets each open
issue to rung 3 with its full history and emits the directive to retry. Nothing is parked
silently.
Acceptance: `task end` with one open issue prints the retry directive and the task stays
open.

### R-024 — Rung 8: the goal is not complete; Zee gets a product report
Decisions: D-08, D-09, D-28, D-42, D-43, D-44, D-45.
When the goal-end gate (R-034) finds open issues, `nemesis report --product` writes a
plain-language report, GROUPED by feature and requirement (D-45), of only those findings
ADMITTED to rung 8: what the group is, what was tried at each rung, and three choices
only — drop the feature, change the requirement, accept the risk with a named owner and
date. It never asks Zee to fix code. The goal remains incomplete until he picks and the
pick is recorded (`nemesis issue decide <id>[,<id>...] --choice <drop|change|accept>
--owner <who>`), which closes those issues as `decided` with the record.
`issue decide` is accepted only at rung 8 and only for an ADMITTED finding.

**AMENDED 2026-11-02.** This requirement previously read "The release gate itself moves
every open issue to rung 8". That blanket sweep was the defect: it sent Zee 356 findings in
a 517,926-byte report, 104 of which were about Nemesis's own paperwork, and 0 of which had
ever been moved up a rung by the judge. The gate now promotes a finding to rung 8 only when
ALL THREE admission tests pass — R-055 (lane), R-056 (answerable), R-057 (ladder
exhausted). Everything that fails an admission test stays BELOW rung 8 and stays OPEN, so
the gate remains shut and no check is weakened (D-06, D-07, D-29); what changes is that the
work is named as the MACHINE's, with the exact command, instead of being signed off by Zee.
Acceptance: the product report contains no code and no diff; `issue decide` records the
choice and owner; before rung 8, and for any finding that failed admission, it refuses.

### R-025 — An issue closes only when it is proven fixed
Decisions: D-11.
`nemesis issue close <id>` succeeds only if ALL hold: (1) a reproduction test is registered
on the issue (`issue repro <id> <testFile> <testName>`), exists, and the test runner
reports it passing; (2) the full suite passes in the latest S3; (3) the latest two S3
records are clean (no fail, no skipped); (4) an independent review record (R-032's S6, or
`nemesis review <issue>` which runs the daemon's attacker review of the fix) reports
`confirmed_fixed: true`. Otherwise it refuses and lists exactly which of the four are
missing. The reproduction test is never removed by Nemesis.
Acceptance: `close` without a repro test refuses naming (1); with all four, closes.

### 4.G Stop hook, anti-skip, completion

### R-026 — The watchdog: work with no record behind it is a defect
Decisions: D-24.
The Stop hook reads the session transcript's last turn(s), lists every `Write`/`Edit`/
`NotebookEdit`/`Bash` tool_use on in-scope paths, and checks each has an S2 verdict record
(edits) and an S3 scan record after it. Any unrecorded work opens a `FUN-` issue
("unrecorded edit: <path>") and the hook blocks with the list.
Acceptance: a transcript fixture with one in-scope Write and no records → block naming the
path and a new open issue.

### R-027 — Nothing stops while the ledger is open; goal-complete is refused by the hook
Decisions: D-10, D-24, D-22.
The Stop hook returns `{"decision":"block","reason":<directive>}` while any issue is open,
any task has unfinished steps, or the release gate record (R-034) is missing when the
transcript's last assistant text contains `AUTOPILOT_GOAL_COMPLETE`. The directive lists
open issues (id, severity, rung, next instruction), unfinished steps per task, and the
command to run next. The hook decides from the ledger files, never from the transcript's
claims.
Acceptance: transcript ending with `AUTOPILOT_GOAL_COMPLETE` and one open issue → block;
with a clean ledger and a release-gate record → no block.

### R-028 — The open ledger is re-injected every prompt
Decisions: D-24.
The UserPromptSubmit hook adds context: active goal, current task and its next unfinished
step, open issues with rung instructions, and the last S3 summary. This survives compaction
because it is read from disk each time.
Acceptance: with one open issue, the hook's additionalContext contains the issue id and its
rung instruction.

### R-029 — The step ledger is written only by the CLI, with artifact hashes
Decisions: D-24.
`.nemesis/tasks/<id>/steps.json` has entries S0, S1, S2 (list), S3 (list), S4, S5, S6, S7,
S8 and PFC-a, PFC-c, PFC-d, PFC-e (PFC-b is the ladder) — each `{done, artifact,
sha256, at, by}`. Only `nemesis` CLI commands and hooks (which call the same library) write
it. `nemesis status` prints every task's steps with done/undone.
Acceptance: `status` output lists all 13 step ids per task; editing steps.json by hand to
mark S5 done without an artifact is detected by `status` (hash mismatch → shown as
`tampered`).

### R-030 — Sub-agents are covered
Decisions: D-24, D-02.
Hooks are project-level, so a `developer` agent spawned by `Task()` runs the same hooks on
its edits; the Stop hook's watchdog and the PostToolUse scan use the `transcript_path` /
`cwd` each hook receives, whichever session made the call.
Acceptance: a hook invoked with a sub-agent transcript path still records against the
project's `.nemesis/`.

### R-031 — Nemesis does not stand down for the eternity protocol
Decisions: D-17.
No Nemesis hook consults `_autopilot_eternity.is_eternity_protocol_active` or any eternity
marker. Eternity's own files (R-002 exclusions) are out of scope, so a handoff is never
blocked. Edits to in-scope code during the marker window are judged exactly as at any other
time.
Acceptance: with a live marker fixture present, an in-scope edit still produces a judge
call; a write to `memory/handoffs/x.md` is allowed without a judge call.

### R-054 — A stalled writer ends the loop with the product report, never with completion
Decisions: D-41, D-08, D-28.
The Stop hook counts consecutive turns with zero tool calls (`.nemesis/state/idle.json`).
When the count reaches `stalled_turns` (config, default 6) while the ledger is open, the
hook writes `nemesis report --product` (R-024), appends a `stalled` history event to every
open issue, records a `stop` record with `stalled: true`, resets the count and allows the
stop with an `additionalContext` that says the goal is NOT complete and names the report.
Every earlier turn in the streak is blocked as in R-027. Issues stay open; `on` resumes them.
**AMENDED 2026-11-02 (D-46).** The escape must LATCH. Previously it reset the count and
re-armed, so the whole cycle repeated: 5 cycles, 1780 `stalled` history events and 16
duplicate product reports totalling 8.9 MB, and when the writer made any tool call the
streak reset before reaching the escape, so the hook blocked 9 consecutive turns and Claude
Code's own cap (`CLAUDE_CODE_STOP_HOOK_BLOCK_CAP ?? 8`) had to override it. See R-058.
Acceptance: with one open issue and a text-only transcript, the first `stalled_turns - 1`
Stop calls block and the next one allows with the report written.

### R-055 — Lanes: a finding about Nemesis's own records never reaches Zee
Decisions: D-42, D-08.
`lib/lane.ts` `laneOf(issue)` returns `record` when the finding is about one of Nemesis's
own records and `product` otherwise, testing in this order: a finding whose text says a check
was `SKIPPED` is `record` (the project needs the check to EXIST, which is work, even for a
skipped secrets scan); a finding under checklist `3.4` is `product` wherever its files live,
because a hard-coded credential inside `.nemesis/` is a real credential; otherwise the place the judge
POINTS AT decides: if `where` names a path under `.nemesis/` the finding is `record`, as it is
when every path it lists is under `.nemesis/`, and as checklist `S0`–`S8` is.
**AMENDED 2026-11-02, measured.** The first version let ANY code rule (`3.x`, `Qn`,
`PFC-a..e`) beat the path test. Against the real ledger that sent Zee 17 findings whose only
files live under `.nemesis/`, because the judge had cited rules 3.14, 3.11 and 3.7 while
describing a stale holdout pack and an S1 summary saying "SEVENTEEN tests". None was a
secret. The safety worry behind the wide version does not hold either: `record` does not drop
a finding — it stays OPEN, the gate stays SHUT, and R-034's rotation gate is independent of
lanes. The lane decides WHO, never WHETHER. A `record` finding is never promoted to rung 8 and
`issue decide` refuses it; `repairInstruction()` names the machine command that clears it.
**SHARPENED 2026-11-02, hours later, by the live ledger.** "Every path under `.nemesis/`"
was false for a finding that points at a record AND names a code file, so FUN-180 - pointing
at `.nemesis/holdout/R-019/manifest.json:35`, also naming `_build/headings.test.ts` - came
back `product` and reached Zee. Its own ruling had already said why that was wrong: "one of
Nemesis's own records, not shipping code. There is no code root cause to fix."
Acceptance: an `S2` "unrecorded edit" finding is `record` and `issue decide` refuses it; a
`3.4.2` credential finding inside `.nemesis/` is `product`; a `3.14` finding about a stale
holdout pack inside `.nemesis/` is `record`; a `3.9` finding whose text says `SKIPPED` is
`record`; a finding whose `where` is under `.nemesis/` is `record` even when it also names a
shipping file, and one whose `where` is a shipping file stays `product` even when it also
names a record.

### R-056 — Admission: rung 8 requires an answerable question
Decisions: D-43, D-09.
`lib/lane.ts` `admission(root, issue)` passes only when the issue's task is on record, has a
non-empty title (the FEATURE Zee could drop) and at least one `prd_refs` entry (the
REQUIREMENT he could change). A failure returns the exact missing anchor and the command
that supplies it (`nemesis task prd-refs <task> <R-00x,...>`), because supplying it is
machine work, not a decision.
Acceptance: an issue on a task with no `prd_refs` fails admission and the message names
`task prd-refs`; adding the refs makes the same issue admissible.

### R-057 — Admission: rung 8 requires an exhausted ladder
Decisions: D-44, D-08, D-04.
A finding may be promoted to rung 8 only when its history records, for EACH rung in
`REQUIRED_RUNGS`, either a real attempt at that rung or a judge ruling naming why it cannot
apply (`ladderExhausted()`). The position rungs may not skip the machine's own options. A
failure names the first rung still owed and its instruction. This closes the measured hole:
0 of 356 findings had been moved by the judge, none had reached rung 4, 5 or 6, and 83 went
from rung 1 to Zee in one hop.
**WIDENED AND NARROWED 2026-11-02, hours later, by the live ledger.** Two further holes, both
measured between 20:35:08.936Z and 22:06:15.614Z, after which 356 of 396 open findings had
reached Zee:
1. `REQUIRED_RUNGS` was `[2, 3, 4]`, so the escape SHORTENED the ladder instead of moving
   along it. The machine ruled rung 2 inapplicable 345 times and rung 3 inapplicable 151
   times, with 123 distinct written reasons - sound reasoning, not boilerplate - and its own
   reasons named the route it should have taken: "the sanctioned route is `nemesis task
   redesign R-019 <issue-id> <design-file>` (rung 5)". It is now `[2, 3, 4, 5, 6]`: every rung
   the machine owns, which is what "every automatic rung was tried" says.
2. `rungAttempted` accepted a history line matching "→ n", meaning the issue was MOVED to
   rung n. Task end parks an open finding at rung 3 and writes "7 → 3 (task-end retry with
   full history)", so 345 of 396 findings counted rung 3 as spent with no work done at it.
   Being parked somewhere is not work. Only a real attempt the judge saw counts, plus the
   three rungs the CLI spends itself and records: rung 4 (`second-opinion`, including the
   honest `unavailable` when no provider is configured, without which a project with no
   `second_opinion_provider` could never clear rung 4 at all), rung 5 (`task redesign`) and
   rung 6 (`revert`).
Acceptance: a fresh issue at rung 1 is refused promotion to rung 8 and the refusal names
rung 2; after real attempts are recorded at every rung in `REQUIRED_RUNGS` the same issue is
admitted; a finding parked at rung 3 by task end still owes rung 3; ruling rungs 2, 3 and 4
inapplicable leaves rungs 5 and 6 owed, never Zee.

### R-058 — The Stop hook may not loop
Decisions: D-46, D-41, D-10.
**AMENDED 2026-11-02, same day it was written.** The first version of this requirement said
the hook allows the stop when the directive is byte-identical to the last blocking one and
the writer made no tool call. Both halves were wrong, and the selftest is what showed it:

1. *Byte-identical* would not have caught the real failure. On website-design-okoa/v2 the
   watchdog opened a fresh finding almost every turn, so the directive CHANGED every turn.
   A counter keyed on the text resets on each change and climbs to the host's cap anyway.
2. *No tool call* is R-054's territory. Releasing there at the second block would have
   pre-empted the stalled escape, so the product report would never have been written.

So the hook keeps TWO counters in `.nemesis/state/idle.json` and releases on either:
`same_block_streak` (identical directive repeats — repeating it cannot help) and
`block_chain` (consecutive blocks in ONE Stop chain, driven by `stop_hook_active`, whatever
the text says). Both are capped by `max_identical_blocks` (default 6), which must stay below
Claude Code's own `CLAUDE_CODE_STOP_HOOK_BLOCK_CAP ?? 8`: being overridden by the host ends
the turn with none of the hook's own record of why, which is how the two overrides at
2026-11-01T08:00:00.431Z and 2026-11-02T06:16:08.457Z left nothing behind but a warning.
A release is never a pass — it carries an `additionalContext` saying the goal is NOT complete
and the ledger is NOT cleared, with the count owed by the machine and the count with Zee —
and "goal complete" is still refused while the ledger is open (R-027). Changing the ledger
re-arms the block. The stalled escape (R-054) still fires first for an idle writer, and it
LATCHES on the ledger's own fingerprint instead of re-arming, so an unchanged ledger does not
get a 17th duplicate report.
Acceptance: eight consecutive Stop calls, each with a NEW finding on the ledger so the
directive differs every time, never produce eight consecutive blocks, and the release names
what is owed and by whom; the same holds with an unchanged ledger and `stop_hook_active`
false; an idle writer still reaches R-054's report first.

### R-059 — Sealing a holdout pack never destroys the pack it replaces
Decisions: D-47, D-33, D-29.
`authorTaskHoldout` writes the new pack into `pack.staging-<ts>/`, runs `holdout seal` against
that staging copy with the REAL manifest path (so every protection the sealer already has still
fires), and only then removes `pack/` and renames the staging copy into place. On a refusal the
staging copy is deleted and `pack/` plus `manifest.json` are left byte-for-byte as they were.
It also pre-checks, before writing anything, that every expectation carries the key its own grade
mode is read for: `exit` for `grade.on` `exit-code`, `stdout` for `stdout-json`/`stdout-text`,
with `exit_code` named as the near-miss it is. At task end, a reveal that produced NO verdict is
re-diagnosed against the same test and reported as a PACK defect naming the missing keys, printing
only case ids and key NAMES, never an expected value, so the wall holds (D-33).
**Why it exists.** Measured in that project's own `holdout.jsonl`: five `seal-failed` events, one
for a bad `status` value and four for "manifest already exists — re-sealing changes the
commitment". On every one of them the new files had already overwritten the previously sealed pack
while the manifest still committed to the old hashes, leaving the directory out of step with its
own commitment.
**CORRECTION, 2026-11-04.** The first version of this requirement said four sealed packs on
website-design-okoa/v2 could not grade themselves, with per-pack counts. That was wrong. The
measurement was taken while the window was re-authoring those packs, so `cases.json` and
`expected.json` momentarily held different generations and no id lined up. All four carry real
verdicts (R-045 PASS, R-050 BLOCK, R-052 BLOCK, R-056 BLOCK). An ungradable pack has in fact been
unsealable since 2026-10-31T05:53:55Z, when `acos-holdout-test/scripts/lib/pack.ts`
`assertCasesCoverExpected` gained the per-grade-mode key checks and the unrecognised-key refusal;
verified by sealing a deliberately flat pack, which was refused with no manifest written. The
guarantee named here is the one that was genuinely missing: a refusal must not damage what it
could not replace.
Acceptance: with a pack already sealed and a manifest in place, an author run whose seal is
refused leaves `cases.json`, `expected.json` and `manifest.json` unchanged and leaves no
`pack.staging-*` directory behind; a pack whose expectation lacks its graded key, or writes
`exit_code`, or omits an entry, or carries an orphan entry, is refused before anything is written;
and a sealed pack with no verdict makes task end report a pack defect naming the missing keys.

### R-060 — A reproduction test must be proved to have run
Decisions: D-48, D-25, D-29.
`runScan` credits a registered reproduction (`repro_results[file::name] === true`) only when the
file exists, the name is DECLARED there on a line that declares a test, that line is not
`.skip`/`.todo`/`.failing`/`xit`/`xtest`, the suite passed, and the suite output does not mark it
failed. A DYNAMICALLY named test has no literal name in the source, so `findDynamicDeclaration`
also accepts a declaration whose name PATTERN can produce the registered name: a template literal
title with `${...}` holes, or an `.each` title with printf-style placeholders
(`%s %d %i %f %j %o %p %#`). The static parts are anchored and the holes become wildcards, so a
pattern that cannot produce the name is still refused — and refused with its own message, naming
the dynamic case rather than claiming the test is absent. Raised by the v2-ee session on
2026-11-04: none existed on that project, but a literal-only rule would eventually refuse a real
reproduction, which is the same class of false refusal as the `bun test` output version below. Every refusal records its cause in `repro_reasons`, which `closureCheck` appends to
criterion (1), so a refusal is actionable rather than a dead end.
**Why the evidence comes from the FILE, not the suite output.** Measured 2026-11-04: `bun test`
prints only `1 pass` / `0 fail` on success and names a test only when it fails. Requiring a pass
marker beside the name in the output would therefore block closure on every project using it. A
declared, unskipped test in a file, with the whole suite green, did run and did pass.
**Why it exists.** Found by the v2-ee session on 2026-11-04 in this skill's own `lib/scan.ts`. The
rule had been: the file exists, the suite passed, and no failure line mentions the test name. A
name present nowhere produces no failure line either, so it was credited. FUN-93 on
website-design-okoa/v2 carried a reproduction pointing at an unrelated test and still satisfied
closure criterion (1) — the one check that proves the bug is actually fixed.
Acceptance: a reproduction naming a test that exists in no file is refused and the refusal says
the file contains no such test; a reproduction naming a `test.skip` is refused as never having
run; a reproduction naming a real declared test in a passing suite is credited; a missing file is
refused and named as missing; a name produced by a template-literal title or an `.each` title is
credited; and a name no pattern in the file could produce is refused with the dynamic-name
message.
**Audited on the live project, 2026-11-04 (by the v2-ee session, at this session's request):** all
94 issues carrying a `repro` field pass the new rule — 81 closed, 4 decided, 10 open. So no closure
there was bought by the old loose rule and nothing needed reopening; the fix costs nothing
retroactively and shuts the hole going forward.

### 4.H Task end and goal end

### R-032 — Task end: S4 twice clean, S6 independent review, S7 holdout, S5 record
Decisions: D-22, D-11, D-06.
