### 4.I Daemon, charter, independence, recovery

### R-035 — The Nemesis daemon
Decisions: D-19, D-33, D-15, D-13.
`scripts/nemesis-daemon.ts` serves `~/.acos/nemesis/nemesis.sock` (0600), writes
`~/.acos/nemesis/verdicts.log` (JSONL, append-only) and `requests.log`, keeps a 0600 token,
and for each request spawns `claude -p --model opus` with `--allowedTools Read,Grep,Glob`
(no Bash, no Write, no Task), the prompt on stdin, and the judge-child token in the
environment. There is NO timeout on a verdict. A keep-alive LaunchAgent plist is installed by
`nemesis daemon install`.
Acceptance: `nemesis daemon status` shows the socket and pid; a request round-trips in test
mode; the spawned argv contains the allowed-tools restriction and no `--dangerously-skip-permissions`.

### R-036 — The charter: deny by default against the rulebook
Decisions: D-06, D-07, D-04, D-13.
`scripts/lib/charter.ts` builds the judge prompt: role (Nemesis, the rulebook's judge, not
the machine-harm judge); the twelve questions verbatim; the severity table; principles; the
S0-selected checklists in full and the others by id; the PFC lens definition; the contract
(R-007); the file's open issues and prior verdicts; the duty to assess stuckness from the
history and recommend a rung with a reason; the instruction to read callers/tests/policies
before answering; the exact JSON output shape (R-009) and nothing else. Default is DENY on
any finding of any severity.
Acceptance: the built prompt contains all twelve questions, the four severity rows, the
selected checklist items, and the JSON schema line.

### R-037 — Verdict recovery: find the reason, get a readable answer, then decide
Decisions: D-14.
`scripts/lib/verdict.ts` classifies a judge reply when no JSON verdict parses: `empty`
(no stdout; stderr captured and inspected), `usage_limit` (stderr/stdout matches
limit/quota/rate patterns), `too_long` (E2BIG or the CLI's prompt-length error),
`prose` (text without a JSON object), `malformed` (JSON-like but invalid). Recovery per
class: `empty`/crash → retry via the direct layer, then again with backoff; `usage_limit` →
wait and retry with backoff (bounded by config `judge_retry_waits_ms`, default 3 tries over 10 minutes), then try
the alternate account config dir if configured; `too_long` → write the candidate and
records to a temp file the judge reads and resend a short prompt; `prose` → a second
JSON-only call that extracts the decision from the prose; `malformed` → repair common
faults (trailing commas, fences) then re-parse. Every step is logged with its cause. Only
when all recovery fails does the daemon return `deny` with `reason` naming the class and
the last error. The string "unparseable — allowed" never appears.
Acceptance: `nemesis verdict-parse` fed each fixture class reports the class and the
recovery taken; none yields `allow`.

### R-038 — The same recovery in the Oracle daemon and direct layer
Decisions: D-14, D-31.
`.claude/scripts/oracle/oracle-daemon.ts`: the prompt is passed on stdin; stderr is captured
and logged; the unparseable fallback-to-allow is removed and replaced by the R-037 recovery
(shared logic reimplemented in the daemon file, since the Oracle must not depend on the
Nemesis skill directory); the final failure is a deny that names the class. `_oracle_judge.py`
(the direct layer): same classification and the `prose` and `malformed` recoveries; a final
deny naming the cause.
Acceptance: `oracle-daemon.ts` contains no `fallback: true` allow path; `parseVerdict`
failures route to recovery; the Python direct layer's deny message contains the class name.

### R-039 — Nemesis is never unreachable without saying why
Decisions: D-06, D-19.
Reachability layers mirror the Oracle: socket → auto-start the daemon → direct `claude -p`
call from the hook → retry with backoff → deny naming what broke. Fail closed.
Acceptance: with no daemon and `claude` absent (fixture PATH), the hook denies with a
reason naming the missing binary.

### R-040 — The judge child is exempt from Nemesis hooks and needs no Oracle verdict
Decisions: D-19.
The daemon passes `ACOS_NEMESIS_JUDGE=<token>` to the child; hooks compare it to
`~/.acos/nemesis/token` (0600) and allow instantly on match. The child has no Bash/Write, so
