## 7. Traceability matrix

Generated by `bun ../acos-skill-maker/scripts/trace.ts matrix prd/TRACE.json` — see
`prd/MATRIX.md` (regenerated at every build gate run).

### R-061 — A rung ruled inapplicable needs an independent confirmation
Decisions: D-49, D-44, D-15, D-08.
`nemesis ladder <id> --na <n> --why "<reason>"` writes `rung-na-proposed`, which counts for
NOTHING. `rungRuledNA` in `lib/lane.ts` honours only `rung-na-confirmed`, written after a second
reader — given the finding and the rung but never the first judge's reasoning (D-15) — answers
`{"possible": false}`. The confirmer's charter states that its job is to DEFEND the ladder, and
that the bar is impossibility rather than difficulty: it must answer `possible: true` if the rung
COULD be carried out, even if that looks unlikely to help, is expensive, or resembles something
already tried. A refusal is recorded as `rung-na-refused` and names the rung's own instruction, so
the machine's next move is stated. If the confirmation cannot be obtained at all, the ruling stays
recorded and uncounted and the command exits non-zero — an unreviewed ruling never shortens the
ladder. The legacy `rung-na` event is still honoured so an existing ledger is not retroactively
broken.
**Why it exists.** Measured on website-design-okoa/v2: 496 rulings, reviewed by nobody.

### R-062 — A ladder is exhausted only when the machine spent a real idea
Decisions: D-49, D-44, D-08.
`ladderExhausted` returns `{ ok: false, floor: true }` when nothing is owed but fewer than
`MIN_RUNGS_SPENT` (1) of `REQUIRED_RUNGS` was spent on REAL WORK. `rungSpentOnRealWork` counts
only an attempt the judge actually saw, a second opinion genuinely `received`, a redesign, or a
revert. `admitToRung8` refuses with that reason and names rung 3 — handing the finding with its
written history to a fresh agent — because that is available on any finding. The OWED list still
accepts an `unavailable` second opinion, so rung 4 never deadlocks on a project with no external
provider; only the FLOOR is strict. Nothing deadlocks overall either: rung 3 is always possible,
and ruling it out now needs an independent confirmation that it is impossible (R-061).
**Why it exists.** An escape hatch that can empty the whole list is not an escape, it is the front
door.
**Measured, in two rounds, and the second round matters more.** The first version counted rung 4
as spent the moment `second_opinion` existed, including `status: "unavailable"`. Measured on
website-design-okoa/v2 the same day, with the cut stated exactly because a bare "338" was not
reproducible by a second session: of 447 open findings **400** carry `unavailable`, **338** of the
**376** in the PRODUCT lane carry it, **47** carry no `second_opinion` key, and **ZERO** anywhere
carry `received`, because no external model is configured on that project at all. The 243 and 266
below are both on the product-lane basis. So one auto-recorded
non-event — "we have no second model" — was the entire ladder for **243** of the **266** findings
the gate was admitting. With the floor counting real work only, **admitted to Zee went 266 → 23**,
and all 23 carry genuine work: 12 redesigns, 6 fresh-agent attempts, 3 root-cause fixes, 2 both.
**And it caught this skill's own tests twice.** Round one refused three fixtures that reached Zee
with nothing spent; round two refused four more that had been cleared by the same non-event the
live ledger was using. Both times the fixtures were amended and the floor was not loosened.
**An attempt records the rung the WORK was done at, never a position.** `workRungFor` maps a
finding sitting at 7 or 8 to rung 3, because those are POSITIONS and rung 7's own instruction is
"Retry from rung 3 with the FULL attempt history". Before this, an edit re-judged while a finding
was parked at 8 was stamped `rung: 8` and paid for no required rung — real work credited to a step
that is not work. Three such stamps exist on the measured ledger and all 130 floor-refused
findings on the task in hand sit at position 8.
**A refusal never names a rung the ledger could not credit.** `recordFindings` appends an attempt
only when the finding's task matches the task being worked, so rungs 2 and 3 cannot be paid on a
finding belonging to another task — 117 of the 247 floor-refused on the measured ledger. That is
not a deadlock, because `cmdTaskRedesign(taskId, issueId, file)` and `cmdRevert(id)` take the
target explicitly and carry no rung gate and no current-task gate, so rungs 5 and 6 are payable on
anything. `payableRungs` names the creditable route for the finding in front of it, says plainly
when rungs 2 and 3 would not count, and says how to make rung 3 payable.
**Both of these were found by the `v2-ee` session**, which measured that the refusal text written
minutes earlier named a move its ledger could never record.

### R-063 — A recurring reason must become a reviewed policy
Decisions: D-49, D-25, D-37.
`naReasonUses` counts, across the whole ledger, how many FINDINGS a normalized reason has already
ruled a rung inapplicable on. At `na_reason_cap` (default 3) `cmdLadderNA` refuses and names the
policy commands. `normalizeReason` lower-cases, replaces issue ids, requirement ids and digits
with placeholders and keeps the first 14 words, so re-wording does not reset the count —
re-wording is how 123 "distinct" reasons covered 496 rulings on the measured ledger.
**Why it exists.** A judgement made three times is a standing rule. A standing rule belongs in one
reviewed, vetoable record, not in a 497th history entry nobody will ever audit.

### R-064 — A policy may only pick a rung the machine owns
Decisions: D-49, D-06, D-07, D-11.
`policyShapeProblems` in `lib/policy.ts` refuses any policy whose `then_rung` is not one of
`POLICY_RUNGS` (2, 3, 4, 5, 6), and the `Policy` type carries NO field that could close, decide,
drop, accept or rule a finding N/A — so the guarantee is structural, not a check that can be
forgotten. The same function also refuses a policy with no written rule, reasoning or EVIDENCE
(20 characters each), a matcher that would select everything, a pattern that is not a valid
regular expression, a matcher too NARROW to catch the case it says motivated it, and a matcher too
WIDE — one that also catches the near-miss the policy itself states it must not. D-11's four
closure criteria are untouched: a reproduction test that passes, a green suite, two clean scans,
an independent reviewer, for every finding, every time.
**Why the too-wide check exists.** On 2026-11-02 my own lane fix was written one step too wide and
reclassified 17 findings it was never meant to touch. A rule is only as good as the near-miss it
declines.

### R-065 — A policy is inert until independently approved, and Zee's veto walks it back
Decisions: D-49, D-15, D-25, D-28, D-37.
A proposed policy has `status: "proposed"` and `applyPolicies` ignores it entirely.
`nemesis policy review <POL-n>` asks a separate reader — which did not write the policy and has
not seen its author's reasoning — to approve or reject it, and is told to reject a matcher wider
than its evidence justifies, evidence that does not support the rule, a rung wrong for the class,
or a rule that makes work disappear instead of doing the right work faster. Only approval sets
`status: "active"`. Every application appends `{at, issue, from_rung, to_rung}` to the policy and
a line to the issue's history and to `records/policy.jsonl`. `nemesis policy veto <POL-n> --why`
sets `vetoed` permanently and restores every still-open finding the policy moved to the rung it
was on before — so Zee's veto is a real undo, not just a stop. A policy only ever moves a finding
UP the ladder, so two policies can never fight over one finding.
**Why it exists.** Zee, 2026-11-04: "it has to be best judgement with checks and balances." An
unreviewed standing rule is one model's opinion applied at scale.

### R-066 — A security finding is never a question for Zee
Decisions: D-50, D-06, D-08, D-28.
`isSecurityFinding(i)` is `i.lens === "PSC"` — the Prime Security Checks lens. `admission` refuses
such a finding before anything else is considered, `cmdIssueProductQuestion` refuses to let one be
claimed as his, and `cmdIssueDecide` refuses to decide one BEFORE it checks the rung, because
being a security finding is decisive and permanent while "not at rung 8 yet" is a position that
will change — naming the position would send the machine to the release gate to promote something
that can never be promoted.
**Why.** Zee, 2026-11-04: *"I care about functionality and security. As long as a system functions
the way I want and there is no security laps in any way, I am good… It is the systems job to code
to ensure the functionality and the security."* His answer to every security finding is already on
record, so none of them has a question left in it — only work.
**Measured.** 46 of 464 open findings on website-design-okoa/v2 are security findings, and none of
them can now reach him by any route.

### R-067 — Nothing reaches Zee without a confirmed functionality or design question
Decisions: D-50, D-08, D-15, D-43, D-44.
`hasConfirmedProductQuestion(i)` requires `i.product_question.confirmed.agreed === true`, written
by `cmdIssueProductQuestion` after a reader that never spoke to the claiming judge agrees. That
reader's charter states that its job is to DEFEND THE OWNER'S ATTENTION, that its default answer
is "machine work", and that a bug, a crash, a wrong result, a missing check, a performance
problem, a document or comment or test description contradicting the code or another document
(including a stale count or date), and anything it is unsure about are all machine work however
often they recur. A refused claim is recorded and PERMANENT — the refusal says "no re-claim
changes that". An unobtainable confirmation leaves the claim recorded and uncounted.
**The test runs LAST**, after Nemesis's own paperwork (D-42), security (D-50), the anchors (D-43)
and the ladder (D-44, D-49), so a refusal always names the machine's next MOVE rather than a claim
to make. Putting it first made every refusal read "claim it if you think it is Zee's", which is
the wrong instruction when it is not.
**Why a count could never have been the answer.** Zee: *"Now it is 23, if I do a large enough job,
it could become 230."* Of the 23 that D-49 left, 2 were security and about twelve of the rest were
one document contradicting another; none was a functionality or design change. A gate keyed on
KIND admits only as many findings as the PRODUCT has genuine change questions, which does not
scale with how much code was written.
**Measured.** Of 464 open findings, **0** reach Zee: 74 paperwork, 46 security, 221 with nothing
yet tried, 102 still owing a rung, and 21 that cleared every other gate and are refused precisely
because they are not functionality or design questions.

### R-068 — Rung 8 offers two choices, and "accept the risk" does not exist
Decisions: D-50, D-06, D-29, D-45.
`cmdIssueDecide` refuses `--choice accept` with Zee's own words as the reason, and accepts only
`drop` and `change`. The grouped product report prints two choices and never the third. The help
text offers `--choice drop|change`. The `Issue["decision"]["choice"]` type still *reads* `accept`
so an old record is not corrupted, but no new one can be written.
**Why.** The third choice existed solely so Zee could sign off a risk, and he has ruled he never
will: *"there is no security laps in any way"*. A choice nobody will ever take is not a choice; it
is a hole with a name.
**Measured before removing it.** Of 88 decisions ever recorded on website-design-okoa/v2, 88 were
`drop` and 0 were `accept`, so nothing historical depended on it.
**The release gate is strictly tighter as a result, not looser.** A secret finding used to be
acceptable, which took it off the open list; now it must actually be fixed. R-034 still blocks the
release until a rotation is recorded, including for a secret finding that is already closed, and
that is checked.

### R-069 — A refused re-author puts the previous sealed manifest back
Decisions: D-51, D-47, D-26, D-33.
`reauthorHoldout(root, cfg, taskId)` is the only route by which a task's holdout manifest is
re-authored. It renames `manifest.json` to `.spent-<ts>.json`, calls `authorTaskHoldout`, and if no
`manifest.json` exists afterwards it renames the spent file BACK, says so in its return value, and
records `reauthor-refused` with `restored_previous_manifest`. If authoring throws, the same restore
runs before the error is re-thrown. A successful re-author leaves the old manifest spent, which is
R-033 unchanged. Both callers — `nemesis task holdout <id> --new` and `cmdTaskRedesign` — use it,
and neither touches the manifest itself any more.
**Why it exists.** `authorTaskHoldout` never throws; it RETURNS a refusal string ("NOT authored",
"NOT sealed"). Both callers spent the manifest first, so a refusal left the task with a pack and no
manifest, making the graded verdict of the pack actually committed to unreachable.
**How it was found, and the generalisable part.** By watching the `v2-ee` session work around it by
hand: *"I copied the graded manifest and pack aside first, per the rule that --new spends the
manifest before it authors."* A workaround a careful operator has to remember is a defect, not a
rule. It is the SAME defect as D-47/R-059 — a destructive step before a fallible one — which was
fixed inside the sealer and left standing in both callers. **When a defect has that shape, fix
every caller, not only the layer where it surfaced.**

### R-070 — A kept prior design is numbered from the highest, and never overwritten
Decisions: D-52, D-26, D-18.
`cmdTaskRedesign` keeps the outgoing design as `design-review.v<N>.yaml`, where `N` is
`Math.max(...numbers already used) + 1` — never the COUNT of matching files. If that name somehow
exists the copy is REFUSED with a message naming the file, rather than performed. A design whose
opening line hand-types a version number draws a one-line NOTE in the command's output; the note
never blocks, and an already-archived design is never edited.
**Why it exists.** Counting collides the moment anything is removed or renamed — with v1..v15
present, count+1 is 16 and is safe, but delete v3 and count+1 is 15, which exists, and
`copyFileSync` overwrites it silently. A stored prior design is a permanent record: `steps.json`
holds the sha256 of the S1 artifact actually installed, so an overwritten archive cannot be
reconstructed. Counting is also blind to any sibling outside the pattern, and one exists in the
wild (`design-review.v5-namespace.yaml`).
**How it was found, and the generalisable part.** Answering the `v2-ee` session's question about
why two archives both open `# S1 v14 - ...`. Measuring the live R-019 archive to answer it found
SIX of sixteen headers naming the wrong version. Leaving those headers untouched is correct — the
archive is the record and its hash is pinned — so the fix belongs where the name is DERIVED, plus a
warning so no new design hand-types a number it does not control. **A count is not an identity**:
the same substitution produced D-44/D-49 (a count of findings as the admission gate) and D-50 (the
KIND must gate, not the count).

### R-071 — A killed re-author is recovered, not only a refused one
Decisions: D-53, D-51, D-47, D-33.
`reauthorHoldout` writes `reauthor.inflight.json` — naming the exact `.spent-<ms>.json` to put
back — BEFORE it renames `manifest.json` away, and clears it on success, on refusal and on throw.
`recoverInterruptedReauthor(root, taskId)` runs at the start of every re-author and in
`nemesis status`, and puts the named file back when there is no `manifest.json`. It refuses while
the marker's pid is still alive, records `reauthor-recovered`, and when the named file is absent
it says the task has NO commitment rather than pretending otherwise.
**Why it exists.** D-51's restore runs in a `catch` and a post-return check, both in-process, so a
SIGKILL reaches neither and leaves a pack with no commitment and no record.
**The trap.** Restoring BEFORE the kill is destructive: the sealer throws when a manifest exists
(`acos-holdout-test/scripts/holdout.ts:106`) and nemesis passes no `--force`, so a manifest put
back under a living author turns a success into a refusal. Kill first, then restore; spent files
are never deleted, so nothing is lost by that order.
**Generalisable.** A recovery path living in the same process as the thing it recovers from is not
a recovery path. **Known limit:** this prevents the next occurrence and cannot repair a task
killed before any marker existed; a commitment is never guessed.

### R-072 — A release stays released for the rest of the host's Stop chain
Decisions: D-54, D-46, D-42.
The Stop hook keeps `block_chain` across every branch that does not block. The cap release
writes the current count back rather than zero, and the stalled-writer branch carries
`idle.block_chain` through untouched. Because the next turn computes `block_chain + 1` while
`stop_hook_active` is true, a cap release is sticky for the remainder of the chain. When the
host's chain ends, `stop_hook_active` goes false, the count falls back to 1, and blocking
resumes.
**Why it exists.** Both branches reset a counter the HOST was still counting, so a release let
one turn through and blocking restarted from 1. The host reached nine consecutive blocks on
website-design-okoa/v2 and overrode the hook.
**How it is tested, and why the old test could not catch it.** The existing chain test ran
exactly `HOST_CAP` turns; the second run of blocks starts after that window. R-072 runs
`HOST_CAP * 2` turns in one unbroken chain. It identifies the cap release by its message,
because a stalled-writer turn is also a non-block and a stricter assertion would claim a
guarantee this hook does not make, and it asserts what the host measures: the longest
consecutive block run stays under `HOST_CAP`.

### R-073 — A comment-wording finding is recorded but never denies
Decisions: D-55, D-50, D-44.
The judge charter forbids findings about a comment's grammar, wording, style, tone or
precision. Its only test for a comment is whether another agent would be led to do the wrong
thing. A misleading comment blocks only inside a document that work is authored FROM (S1, S0,
spec, PRD, holdout README). Elsewhere the judge sets `comment_only: true`, `parseVerdict`
keeps the finding but excludes it from the deny decision, and the edit lands. A finding
without the flag behaves exactly as before, and findings about CODE are unchanged.
**Why it exists.** Measured on website-design-okoa/v2: 553 edits judged, 345 denied (62%),
2.0 findings per denial, so each attempt produced 1.24 new findings. Above 1.0 a queue grows
without limit, and that ledger went 42 open to 789 and never fell. 515 of 764 open findings
(67%) were comment wording; 85 (11%) were a test behaving wrong.
**Generalisable.** When a queue grows without bound, measure the per-attempt multiplier
first. If it exceeds 1.0, only the failure rate or the findings-per-failure can fix it, and
any change that moves neither is decoration.

### R-074 — The judge's advice is enforced before a judge call is spent
Decisions: D-56, D-04, D-49.
After `BRAKE_AT` (3) consecutive denials on one file in which the judge said `stuck` with a
recommended rung of 2 or higher, `pre-tool.ts` refuses further edits to that file in
`brakeRefusal`, BEFORE `requestVerdict`. The refusal quotes the rung instruction and the
judge's own reason, and names the exact command that clears it. A not-stuck denial breaks the
chain; a judged-clean edit deletes the record. `nemesis rung ack <id> --action
<root-cause|fresh-agent|second-opinion|redesign|revert> --why "..."` frees the file, is capped
at `ACK_LIMIT` (2) per file until a clean edit lands, and is written to the issue history so
the next judge reads the claim. `nemesis rung brakes` shows what is held and why.
**Why it exists.** Measured on website-design-okoa/v2: of 553 judged edits, 227 (41%) were
attempted after the judge had already flagged `stuck`, and 9 passed - a 4% pass rate against
61% when not stuck. 124 were on one file. At 2.0 findings per denial those ~218 doomed edits
made ~436 findings, about 44% of every finding on the project. `applyAssessment` raised the
rung on all of them and nothing read the label.
**Why it cannot deadlock.** `nemesis task redesign` (rung 5), `nemesis revert` (rung 6) and
`nemesis rung ack` all run through the CLI, which this hook allows unjudged, and each takes its
target explicitly with no rung gate. Only this one file is held; the root cause rung 2 asks for
usually lives in another, which stays editable.
**Generalisable.** A recommendation that nothing refuses is a comment. D-49 found that an
escape with no check on it becomes the front door; this is the same shape inverted - an
instruction with no check on it is ignored, however correct it is.

### R-075 — Every stop record says whether the writer worked, and the progress write drops no field
Decisions: D-57, D-54, D-46.
All four `appendRecord(root, "stop", ...)` calls in `hooks/stop.ts` carry `streak`,
`last_turn_tool_calls` and `transcript` (the `transcript_path` the hook was handed, or `null`). The per-turn progress write spreads the prior idle state
(`writeIdle(root, { ...idle, streak, ... })`) instead of passing three fields to a full
overwrite, so `last_block_sha`, `latched_sha`, `same_block_streak` and `block_chain` survive a
turn that returns early.
**Why it exists.** The RELEASE branch recorded neither field, and that is the branch where "was
the writer working or spinning?" is the question. Reading the absence as a zero produced a
confident, wrong report to Zee on 2026-11-09: 8 releases at 11:57:53-11:58:18Z were described as
turns with no tool calls, while `idle.json` held `last_turn_tool_calls: 4` and every blocked
record in the window carried `streak: 0`. Separately, the three-field progress write deleted the
host-chain count and D-46's report latch from disk on every turn; only the later branches writing
them back from memory hid it, and the `!d.open` early return does not.
**The `transcript` field (added 2026-11-09).** `streak` and the tool-call count say whether the
writer worked; they cannot say WHO. A watcher reading these records to tell a working session
from a stopped one must check that session's own transcript file, and a project folder can hold
several sessions — the OKOA website project holds a peer alongside the writer — so the liveness
of the wrong one would vouch for a dead writer. At 22:05Z a quiet alarm fired on a writer whose
transcript had been appended to that very minute. The path is now recorded rather than guessed.
**How it is tested.** R-075 takes the `!d.open` path deliberately (a fixture with no task and no
findings) and asserts a planted chain, latch and block sha all survive, that the record agrees
with the file, that all four stop records carry both fields, and that each one records
`input.transcript_path` rather than leaving the session unnamed. Two positive controls were run:
restoring the three-field write fails four assertions and leaves `idle.json` holding only
`{streak, last_turn_tool_calls, updated_at}`; stripping the release record's activity fails one.
**Generalisable.** When a record omits a field on one branch only, that branch will be
misdiagnosed, and the first reader to do it will be confident. An absent field and a zero field
are identical bytes and opposite facts.

### R-076 — A focused review judges its focus; a drifted line is the same finding
Decisions: D-58, D-52, D-11.
`runReview` returns `confirmed: focus ? !!focusVerdict : !!r.confirmed_fixed && findings.length === 0`.
With a focus issue the verdict is that issue's verdict alone; findings about anything else are
filed and do not veto it. Without a focus the review is about the whole task and still requires
zero findings. `buildReviewCharter` asks for `focus_fixed`, `focus_why` and `already_filed`, states
the separation high up, and forbids re-filing a defect already on the record list.
`recordFindings` de-duplicates on the FILE of `where`, not `file:line`, and writes the moved line
into the issue history as `re-found [now at X, was Y]`.
**Why it exists.** Measured on website-design-okoa/v2 2026-11-09: ten review runs, zero confirmed,
87 of 156 new findings that day opened BY a review (56%), and 117 of 117 closes required a
confirmed review. SEC-10 had a passing reproduction and its third review said "SEC-10's own
complaints are resolved on disk" while refusing over a one-line citation drift and a sentence it
itself said was already filed as SEC-97 and FUN-951.
**How it is tested.** R-076's first test gives the reviewer a focus verdict of true plus a
neighbouring finding and asserts the close is confirmed, the neighbour is still filed, and the
reviewer's evidence and already-filed list both surface. Its second test records the same defect
at `:488` then `:489` and asserts one finding with both lines in its history, while a different
checklist in the same file stays separate. Positive controls: restoring the veto clause makes the
CLI print "NOT confirmed — the focus issue is fixed"; restoring the line in the key opens SEC-2
beside SEC-1.
**Generalisable.** When a verdict about one thing is vetoed by facts about another thing, the gate
measures the neighbourhood rather than the subject, and on any system with a backlog it never
opens. This is D-55's multiplier arithmetic applied to the closing path instead of the writing
path.

### R-077 — A closed finding's proof is re-verified at the release gate
Decisions: D-59, D-11, D-57.
`cmdReleaseGate` calls `reproEvidence(root, i.repro!, suite)` for every finding with
`status === "closed"` that carries a reproduction, and refuses naming each failure, its close
date and the reason. `reproEvidence` is the body of `runScan`'s reproduction loop, lifted out so
the gate and the scan share one rule and cannot drift. It catches the three realistic ways a
proof evaporates, all visible in the file: the file is deleted, the named test is removed, or it
is marked skip/todo/failing. A missing test runner yields `ok: false` with that stated, never a
silent pass. Refusals are recorded as `proof-rotted` in `records/release.jsonl`.
**Why it exists.** Every live `runScan` caller passes `listIssues(root, true)` — open findings
only — so a closed finding's proof was checked once and never again, while `lib/report.ts` kept
telling Zee it "was Closed after the reproduction test passed". Measured on
website-design-okoa/v2 2026-11-09: 177 closed findings rest on 13 proof files, with
`_build/headers.test.ts` carrying 34. Deleting that one file would un-prove 34 closed findings
and the gate would still have opened.
**How it is tested.** R-077 closes a finding on a real declared passing test, then deletes the
proof file (refuse, naming the finding and its close date), then restores the file with the test
`.skip`ped (refuse, saying skipped), then restores it properly (no longer the complaint). It also
asserts the gate reuses `reproEvidence` rather than a second copy of the rule. Positive controls:
restoring `listIssues(root, true)` fails six assertions; neutering the filter fails five.
**Generalisable.** A gate that checks a condition once and records the result has stored a claim,
not a guarantee, and the claim decays silently while the record asserts it in the present tense.
### R-078 — `fixing` is claimed per finding, from the diff's own changed lines
Decisions: D-60, D-10, D-11.
`markFixing(root, rel, diff, verdictId)` replaces R-017's file-wide loop. `changedOldLines(diff)`
walks each hunk BODY to get the OLD line ranges the edit really changed (the `@@ -a,b` header
counts the three context lines `diff -u` prints either side, so trusting it would double the
window). `fixClaims` then claims a finding only when `lineIsInChange(i.where, ranges)` — its
recorded line inside a changed range, within 3 lines of drift — and only while at most
`LINE_CLAIM_CAP = 8` findings qualify. A finding with no line recorded has only "same file", so
it is claimable only when at most `FILE_CLAIM_CAP = 2` findings are open on that file. When
nothing qualifies, ONE `fix-claim-declined` entry goes to `records/issues.jsonl` carrying the
path, the verdict, how many were open and why — a file-wide edit reported once instead of N
per-finding claims.
**Why it exists.** On 2026-11-09 one clean edit (v-1794258005426-e8bb1abf, 21:00:50) on
`_build/headings.test.ts` flipped 334 findings to `fixing` at once, and 1801 of the 1945
`fix-landed` events ever recorded sit on that one file. Only 70 of the 334 carried a line number,
so 264 of them could never have had evidence about themselves. `fixing` weakens no gate (`isOpen`
counts it as open, nothing else reads it), but the inflated count was relayed to Zee twice as
evidence that closes were about to arrive, so the cost was a false report, not a cosmetic one.
**How it is tested.** R-078 builds a 40-line file carrying 16 open findings — 3 at the line about
to be edited, 10 far away, 3 with no line — plus a second file with one lineless finding. A
one-line edit claims exactly the 3; a whole-file rewrite claims nothing and writes one
`fix-claim-declined` with `open_on_file: 13`; the lone finding on the other file is still claimed,
so the ordinary case is not collateral damage. It also asserts the parser reads the body
(`[{"start":20,"end":20}]`) where the header would have said `@@ -17,7 +17,7 @@`, and that the old
loop is gone from the hook rather than bypassed. A second test exercises `fixClaims` directly with
no fixture. Positive controls: removing both caps fails both tests; making the line test always
answer yes fails both; restoring the old file-wide loop fails the end-to-end test while the
pure-function test correctly still passes.
**Generalisable.** One piece of evidence cannot support N claims, and a match on the container is
not a match on the thing.

